---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "Tonga Room & Hurricane Bar",
  servesCuisine: ["Polynesian", "Asian Fusion"],
  priceRange: "$$",
  telephone: "+1-415-772-5278",
  url: "https://www.tongaroom.com",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "950 Mason St",
    addressLocality: "San Francisco",
    addressRegion: "CA",
    postalCode: "94108",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Historic tiki bar and restaurant inside the Fairmont San Francisco, built in the hotel's former 75-foot indoor pool, with Polynesian-Asian fusion food, classic tiki cocktails, live music, and indoor rainstorms.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Tonga Room Menu Guide (2026) — Prices, What to Order | Hotel Menu Guide"
  description="An independent guide to the Tonga Room & Hurricane Bar in San Francisco: menu items with reported prices, tiki cocktails, the cover charge, hours, and visiting tips. Dish prices from a published 2024 menu."
  path="/tonga-room-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/tonga-room-dining.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/tonga-room-dining.webp"
      alt="Illustration of the Tonga Room's tiki bar with its lagoon pool in San Francisco"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · San Francisco
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      Tonga Room &amp; Hurricane Bar — Menu Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      Deep inside the Fairmont San Francisco sits one of the strangest and most beloved dining
      rooms in America: a tiki bar built inside the hotel's former 75-foot swimming pool, with
      a lagoon where the deep end used to be, a band playing from a floating boat, and a
      thunderstorm that rolls through every half hour. Anthony Bourdain called it "the greatest
      place in the history of the world." The food is Polynesian-Asian fusion bar fare, the
      drinks are classic tiki, and the whole thing has been running since 1945. Below is our
      independent guide to what it costs and how to plan a night here — written in our own
      words, with prices reported from published sources, not copied from a menu.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">950 Mason St, San Francisco, CA 94108 (inside Fairmont San Francisco)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Cuisine</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Polynesian / Asian-fusion tiki bar</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Price level</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">$$ — casual; $20 cover once live music starts</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Hours</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Wed–Thu 5–10 PM; Fri–Sat 5–11 PM; live music from 7 PM; 21+ after 10 PM</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Reservations</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Limited bookings via the Tablz app; first-come first-served otherwise</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (415) 772-5278</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>Food</h2>
      <p>
        The menu is tiki-bar comfort: shareable starters, poke, and a few heartier plates. The
        prices below come <strong>from the official Fairmont menu PDF published around March
        2024</strong> — they're dated and may have changed, so treat them as a guide to the
        price band, not today's numbers. Descriptions below are our own paraphrase of the
        dishes, not the menu's wording.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line my-6 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-parchment text-left">
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Dish</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-right">Reported price (2024 menu)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line bg-white">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Pork siu mai — steamed pork-and-shrimp dumplings</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$15</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Coconut shell spicy tuna poke — ahi tuna with macadamia, taro chips</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$18</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Curried crab won tacos — dungeness crab, yellow curry, pickled daikon</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$16</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Tonga pupu platter — the big shareable spread</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$66</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Tonga grilled ribeye</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$54</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Marinated Korean short rib</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$26</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Vegetable pot stickers — cabbage, black mushroom, ginger</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$14</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Peel &amp; eat prawns — wok-fried with jalapeño, garlic, yuzu mayo</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$12</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Our take: eat here like it's a bar with great snacks, not a restaurant with a bar. The
        pupu platter is built for a group; the poke and the crab tacos are the two dishes that
        justify the trip on their own.
      </p>

      <h2>Cocktails</h2>
      <p>
        The drinks program is classic tiki, and it's the main event for most visitors. Reported
        signature cocktails include the <strong>Mai Tai 1944</strong>, the <strong>Zombie</strong>
        (made with five rums), the <strong>Tiki Hunter</strong>, and the <strong>Wiki
        Wiki</strong>. <strong>Cocktail prices are unverified</strong> — a January 2025 review
        noted $2–4 per-drink increases, which suggests the list has moved since the 2024 food
        menu was published. Ask for current prices at the bar.
      </p>

      <h2>The experience</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part no other hotel restaurant can match. The Tonga Room opened in
        <strong>1945</strong>, converted from the hotel's 75-foot indoor swimming pool — "The
        Plunge," built in 1929 — by MGM set designer Mel Melvin into a tropical lagoon. Since
        the 1950s, an indoor rainstorm — thunder, lightning, rain over the water — has rolled
        through roughly every 30 minutes, and it's still running. The house band plays from a
        floating boat stage in the middle of the lagoon. It's been called the most unusual
        restaurant in California, and the Bourdain line — "the greatest place in the history of
        the world" — is on the official site for a reason: people who go once talk about it
        for years.
      </p>

      <h2>What we'd order</h2>
      <p>If we had one night here, this is how we'd play it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>To drink:</strong> start with the Mai Tai 1944 — it's the tiki canon — then let the bartender talk you into a Zombie if you're staying a while.</li>
        <li><strong>To eat:</strong> the curried crab won tacos and the coconut-shell poke to start; the pupu platter if you're three or more.</li>
        <li><strong>Time it for the storm.</strong> Get there before 7 PM when the live music starts, settle in, and let the rainstorm roll through mid-drink — that's the whole show.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hours are limited:</strong> Wednesday–Thursday 5–10 PM, Friday–Saturday 5–11 PM. Closed Sunday through Tuesday — don't plan a weekend trip around it without checking.</li>
        <li><strong>The cover:</strong> $20 per person once live music starts at 7 PM, applied to all seated guests regardless of when they arrived. It's waived for registered Fairmont hotel guests.</li>
        <li><strong>Reservations</strong> are limited and handled through the Tablz app; otherwise seating is first-come, first-served — arriving early on Friday or Saturday is the move.</li>
        <li><strong>21+ after 10 PM.</strong> All ages are welcome from 5–10 PM, so families can do the early evening.</li>
        <li><strong>Dress code</strong> is casual — this is a tiki bar, not a tasting room.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Dish prices above are <strong>from a published 2024 menu and may have changed</strong> —
        the restaurant's official site notes the menu is subject to change. Cocktail prices are
        <strong>unverified</strong>. Always confirm current prices with the restaurant before
        visiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to all guides</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
